    exit: { frame: 'RACE', stim: 'Read two descriptions of the same dog. A: "The pup bounded across the lawn, ears flopping, tail wagging like a flag." B: "The mutt lunged across the yard, teeth bared, growling like distant thunder."', q: 'How does word choice create a different tone in each description? Support your answer with evidence.', model: 'R: Word choice creates a different tone in each description of the dog. A: Description A has a playful, happy tone, and Description B has a threatening tone. C: A uses "pup," "bounded," and "tail wagging like a flag," while B uses "mutt," "lunged," "teeth bared," and "growling like distant thunder." E: This shows that positive connotations and a cheerful simile make the dog seem friendly, while negative connotations and a stormy simile make the same dog seem dangerous.', look: ['Names both tones', 'Quotes words from A and B', 'Explains connotation/simile effects'] }
